    "heading": "Warming Up With a Morning Brew",
    "body": "Starting today's Easy puzzle felt just like that first sip of coffee—smooth and satisfying. I immediately looked for the biggest numbers, and that sum target of 10 across cells [1,0] and [1,1] jumped right out at me. Since the highest pip value is 6, the only way to get a 10 with the dominoes provided was using a 5 and another 5. Looking at my tray, I had [0,5] and [5,3]. This meant I had to place them so the 5s landed in those specific spots.\n\nOnce those were in place, the rest of the board started to behave. The equals constraint involving [0,2], [0,3], and [1,2] was the next big clue. Since [1,2] was the other half of my [5,3] domino, it carried a value of 3. That forced [0,2] and [0,3] to also be 3. From there, it was a quick game of matching the remaining dominoes like [2,0] and [3,1] into their slots. It is always a great feeling when the final domino, [0,5], fits perfectly into the last gap at [1,4] and [1,3] while satisfying that less than 2 condition.",
  },
  "medium": {
    "heading": "Finding the Middle Ground Rhythm",
    "body": "The Medium puzzle today definitely stepped up the pace. I spent a good minute staring at the sum targets of 7 scattered around the grid. The trick here was managing the empty cells at [1,3], [2,7], and [3,4]. Since these do not contribute to sums, they actually act as helpful walls that narrow down where a domino can actually sit. I started near the bottom right where cell [4,6] had to be less than 3. This led me to place the [3,1] domino there, which helped satisfy the sum of 7 at [3,5] and [3,6].\n\nWhat really tripped me up for a second was the [5,5] domino. It is such a heavy hitter that it can only go in a few places without blowing past those sum targets. I eventually realized it had to live in a spot where it didn't interfere with the 7s. Once I tucked the larger values into the vertical regions, the smaller dominoes like [0,1] and [1,1] fell into place along the left side. It felt like putting together a jigsaw where the edges are made of math instead of cardboard.",
  },
  "hard": {
    "heading": "Tackling the Friday Heavyweight",
    "body": "Now, the Hard puzzle was a real brain teaser! I found the best way in was by looking at the single-cell sum requirements at the very top. Cell [0,0] needing to be 2 and [0,1] needing to be 5 gave me a massive head start. I knew the domino at those coordinates had to be the [2,5]. Similarly, [0,2] needing a sum of 0 meant it had to be a blank side, which perfectly matched with the [0,6] domino when paired with the greater than 5 constraint at [1,2].\n\nThe real challenge was the middle section with all the equals and less than constraints. I had to be very careful with the dominoes [1,3], [1,1], and [4,1]. The breakthrough came when I noticed the equals constraint at [4,1] and [4,2]. By testing the remaining pairs, I saw that only the [4,1] domino could bridge that gap while keeping the neighboring less than 2 regions happy. It took a bit of back-and-forth, but seeing the whole grid light up at the end made the extra effort totally worth it.",
  },
  "tips": "Try to look for the most restrictive constraints first, like a sum of 12 or 0, because they only have one possible pip combination. Also, pay close attention to the empty cells as they often act as anchors for the dominoes around them. If you get stuck, try working from the corners inward where there are fewer possible domino orientations.",
  "learned": "Today really highlighted how useful the greater than and less than signs are for narrow choices. In the hard puzzle, seeing a greater than 5 immediately tells you that cell must be a 6, which is a huge shortcut. I also noticed that in the medium puzzle, the empty cells are not just decorative—they are essential for defining the boundaries of the longer sum regions.",
